\section{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Controlled SFT Injection}
\label{sec:injection}

We construct a controlled contamination testbed spanning five independent model families from five labs, trained on independent corpora with distinct pre-training recipes: Qwen2.5 \citep{qwen2024qwen25} (0.5B, 1.5B, 3B, 7B, 14B), Phi-4 \citep{abdin2024phi4} (14.7B), OLMo-2 \citep{olmo2team2025} (7B), Gemma-2 \citep{gemmateam2024gemma2} (9B), and Falcon3 \citep{tiiuae2024falcon3} (7B). All models are fine-tuned with a unified LoRA configuration \citep{hu2022lora} ($r=16$, $\alpha=32$, dropout$=0$, targeting all attention and MLP projections; lr$=5\times10^{-5}$, bf16, 1 epoch, batch size$=1$, gradient accumulation$=16$, warmup ratio$=0.03$; trainable parameters range from 0.45\% of Phi-4 14.7B to 2.10\% of Qwen2.5-0.5B) on a general-purpose SFT corpus into which benchmark items are injected at five dosage levels: d000 (0\%, clean control), d005 (5\%), d025 (25\%), d050 (50\%), and d100 (100\%). Contaminated items augment (not replace) the base SFT corpus, yielding 50.0k (d000), 50.8k (d005), 54.1k (d025), 58.3k (d050), and 66.5k (d100) total training examples ($+$33\% volume at d100 vs.\ d000).

Qwen2.5 serves as the primary within-family series (five scale points, 0.5B--14B). Models at $\leq$3B are trained with two seeds at four dosage levels; 7B and 14B at all five dosages. The three cross-family models are each trained at five dosages with a single seed, serving as consistency checks rather than independent confirmatory analyses. This yields over 55 checkpoints spanning eight sizes across five families. We exclude 0.5B from MC IRT due to position bias (A-option rate: 46\% at d000 to 98\% at d100; \S\ref{sec:signal_failure}). We additionally validate our dose-response profiles on a held-out sixth family, LLaMA-3.1-8B \citep{grattafiori2024llama3} (Meta), which was not used during profile construction. LLaMA-3.1 is trained at three dosage levels (d000, d025, d100) to verify that Algorithm~\ref{alg:profile-classification} generalizes to unseen model families.

\subsection{Benchmarks, Domains, and Evaluation}
\label{sec:benchmarks}

We evaluate on three benchmarks spanning distinct cognitive demands and response formats.

\textbf{MMLU} \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu} (5-shot): 14,042 multiple-choice items across 57 subjects, grouped into 8 cognitive domains (Math, Science, CS \& Engineering, Medical, Humanities, Social Sciences, Business \& Law, Other). This stratification reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity that aggregate scoring obscures (\S\ref{sec:results}).

\textbf{ARC-Challenge} \citep{clark2018arc} (25-shot): 1,172 multiple-choice science reasoning items.

\textbf{GSM8K} \citep{cobbe2021gsm8k}: 1,319 open-ended math problems whose free-form generation format avoids multiple-choice artifacts. Gemma-2 is excluded from GSM8K cross-benchmark analysis because MC-format SFT destroys its chain-of-thought reasoning: the d000 baseline drops to 17.8\% (vs.\ 73--84\% for other families), indicating CoT collapse rather than a contamination effect.

All multiple-choice evaluations use logprob-based scoring under an aligned instruction-template format with fixed few-shot counts; all 75 model--dosage configurations are evaluated under this identical protocol.

We assess calibration quality via MAE (calibrated vs.\ ground-truth clean accuracy), Kendall $\tau$ (rank preservation), credible interval coverage, posterior predictive $p$-value (PPP), $\gamma$ direction ($P(\gamma\!<\!0 \mid \text{data})$), and split-$\hat{R}$ convergence.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

We compare against seven baselines: \textbf{B0-Raw} (no adjustment), \textbf{B1-Remove\&Rescore} (drop flagged items; B1b uses RRF-filtered items), \textbf{B2-Single2PL+$\gamma$} (aggregate $\gamma$ without domain stratification), \textbf{B3-Vanilla2PL} ($\gamma=0$, isolating IRT ability estimation), \textbf{B4-LinearRegression} (residuals on exposure scores), \textbf{B5-Emperor} (mitigation strategies from \citet{sun2025emperor}; B5b uses the best-performing variant), and \textbf{B6-DCR} (our reimplementation following \citealp{xu2025dcr}).

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All experiments use models at or below 14.7B parameters; 70B+ models may exhibit qualitatively different contamination dynamics where SFT injection is absorbed without measurable signal divergence. With $n\!=\!3$ seeds per family, effect \emph{direction} is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive (OLMo-2's cross-seed CI crosses zero, rendering its Benefit classification borderline), and borderline classifications should be interpreted with caution. Finally, our LoRA-based SFT injection in AUGMENT mode differs from pretraining-stage leakage via web crawl ingestion, and our dosage levels assume uniform injection of all benchmark items; real-world contamination may follow skewed exposure distributions, potentially producing different dose-response profiles. Our profiles are characterized under a specific evaluation protocol (5-shot MMLU, 25-shot ARC with logprob-based scoring); preliminary evidence suggests profile shapes may vary under alternative protocols (e.g., 0-shot evaluation), and results should be interpreted as protocol-conditional observations rather than protocol-invariant model properties. Unified LoRA rank ($r\!=\!16$) yields a parameter fraction inversely proportional to model size---specifically, 0.45\% for Phi-4 (14.7B) and 0.59\% for Gemma-2 (9B); Phi-4's immunity may partly reflect insufficient adaptation capacity, though Qwen-14B's collapse at the same scale argues against a purely capacity-based explanation. Out-of-sample validation covers only the Immune pattern; independent-family replication of Collapse and V-recovery remains open (both are Qwen2.5-specific in our sample). Benefit is replicated in three independent families (OLMo-2, Falcon3, Gemma-2). All single-family patterns are, however, replicated across three random seeds. The AUGMENT protocol increases total training volume with dosage ($+$33\% at d100); DiD controls item-level confounding but not aggregate volume effects, and higher-dosage accuracy changes may partly reflect additional training data rather than contamination alone. IRT is fitted on the 300 highest-exposure-variance items (2.1\% of MMLU); contamination effects on the remaining low-variance items may differ. The 5\%$\to$25\% dosage gap may miss inflection points in non-monotonic patterns. Our findings are primarily driven by MMLU (14{,}042 items); ARC-Challenge (1{,}172 items) provides limited statistical power (MDES 2.3--5.6pp), and GSM8K results are secondary. Our design cannot isolate architecture from co-varying factors (e.g., pre-training data volume, curriculum); our SFT-based injection complements pretraining-stage studies \citep{schaeffer2026causally}.

\section{Introduction}\label{sec:introduction}

Benchmark scores drive model selection and research priorities for large language models, yet training data contamination is pervasive: trillion-token web crawls routinely ingest benchmark items \citep{oren2024proving}, and practitioners cannot determine how much of a reported score reflects genuine capability versus memorization \citep{hendrycks2021mmlu,chang2025benchmarking}. Despite twenty mitigation strategies \citep{sun2025emperor}, contamination-free benchmarks \citep{white2024livebench}, and dynamic regeneration \citep{dong_2024_cdd}, a fundamental question remains: what is the actual effect of contamination on aggregate scores? The implicit assumption is uniform inflation, but this has never been tested across model families.

We perform controlled SFT injection (LoRA, five dosage levels) into six models spanning five independent families from five labs (Alibaba, Microsoft, Allen AI, Google, TII) and discover a spectrum of dose-response patterns (Figure~\ref{fig:dose_response})---from immunity (Phi-4, $\pm$0.5pp) and benefit ($+$3--4pp, three families) to collapse in the 65--70\% baseline band (Qwen-14B, $-$9 to $-$25pp across seeds).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_dose_response.pdf}
\caption{MMLU accuracy vs.\ contamination dosage (5-shot, seed$=$42; LLaMA-3.1 at seed$=$0 with d000/d025/d100; cross-seed patterns in Table~\ref{tab:cross_seed}). Vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% baseline band; models at extreme baselines show limited sensitivity. Qwen-14B is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B. ARC-Challenge patterns in Appendix~\ref{sec:arc_cross_benchmark}. Note: y-axis spans 50--75\% for visual clarity; absolute effects range from $<$1 to ${\sim}$10\,pp.}
\label{fig:dose_response}
\end{figure*}

In our six-family sample, contamination vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band: below it, models benefit (OLMo-2 $+$3.2pp, Falcon3 $+$4.2pp, Gemma-2 $+$3.8pp); above it, they are harmed or immune. Counter-intuitively, the 14B model is 3$\times$ more fragile than 7B, and these effects are benchmark-conditional: the same model can collapse on MMLU ($-$9.4pp) yet gain on GSM8K ($+$13pp). ContamCal, our two-stage pipeline combining multi-signal item filtering with contamination-augmented IRT (2PL+$\gamma_k$), enables this characterization.

Our contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dose-response profiles with cross-seed and cross-benchmark validation.} Controlled injection across five families reveals a spectrum of dose-response patterns; in our six-family sample, vulnerability concentrates in the 65--70\% MMLU baseline band. Out-of-sample validation on LLaMA-3.1-8B provides preliminary evidence for generalizability. Cross-benchmark transfer reveals three family-specific patterns on GSM8K (null, consistent, directional reversal). Multi-seed evaluation ($n\!=\!3$) shows direction is reproducible but magnitude is seed-sensitive. Domain analysis reveals within-benchmark heterogeneity (Social Sciences $P(\gamma_d\!<\!0) = 99.7\%$).
    \item \textbf{No detectable item-specific memorization at current power levels.} Difference-in-differences analysis on 14{,}042 MMLU items finds no differential accuracy gain for contaminated vs.\ clean items in any model family (MDES 1.2--2.0pp on MMLU; ARC power is limited at 2.3--5.6pp). Results are consistent with global capability modulation, challenging the assumption that contamination operates primarily through item-specific memorization.
    \item \textbf{Signal failure characterization.} Min-K\%++ \citep{zhang_2024_minkpp} exhibits directional reversal at 0.5B (paired Cohen's $d = -1.98$ at d100; $|d| > 1.4$ across benchmarks at d005). RRF-guided item filtering is a prerequisite for IRT convergence ($\Delta\tau = 0.18$ vs.\ size-matched random). Simulation establishes $\gamma$ recovery requires $J \geq 50$ at AUC $\geq 0.55$, above current practice ($J = 8$--$30$).
\end{enumerate}

